Unambiguous translation of CUG as serine in
C. albicans
yeast and hyphal growth forms
To investigate whether the reported CUG ambiguity is strain or growth form dependent, we tested five strains, DSM70014, SC5314 (clade 1), and three genetically distinct clinical isolates EU0006 (clade 2), EU0009 (clade 12), and EU0075 (clade 4) [
25
]. The latter four strains were grown in yeast and hyphal growth form. All analyses resulted in the same unambiguity of the CUG translation (Table
1
, Fig.
1
).
Discussion
Our unbiased, statistical evaluation of proteome data suggests that CTG-clade yeasts do not translate the CUG codon ambiguously in vivo. Although there is a measurable level of CUG mistranslation, this level is (i) similar to that of the leucine CUC and serine UCC codons, (ii) similar in six CTG-clade species covering different substitutions of the conserved guanosine nucleotides adjacent to the CAG anticodon in
